<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mikulčice</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mikulčice is a village in South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, near which lies one of the largest fortified centres of Great Moravia, possibly its capital. Excavations since 1954 have uncovered an acropolis with a palace, at least twelve churches and some 2,500 graves, many rich in jewellery and weapons, from the ninth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mikulcice.jpg" alt="Mikulčice" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This image is a work by Mercy. When reusing, please credit me as: Mercy from Wikimedia Commons. … · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mikul%C4%8Dice_-_kostel_Nanebevzet%C3%AD_Panny_Marie_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mikulčice is a municipality and village in Hodonín District in the South Moravian Region, with about 2,000 inhabitants. It lies on the Kyjovka River in the flat Lower Morava Valley, about 6 kilometres southwest of Hodonín and 52 kilometres southeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a>, on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. The Czech-Slovak border is formed here by the Morava River. The municipality has two parts, Mikulčice and Těšice, with 974 and 921 inhabitants in the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the 6th until the 10th century a Slavic fortified settlement existed 3 kilometres southeast of the modern village, at the site called Mikulčice-Valy. It was one of the main centres of the Great Moravian Empire and plausibly its capital city. Excavations unearthed the remnants of twelve churches, a palace and more than 2,500 graves, including a horse burial.</p><p>The first written mention of the village is from 1141, and its Church of the Assumption of the Virgin Mary was first mentioned in 1353. At the beginning of the 15th century a fortress stood here, probably destroyed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussite-wars">Hussite Wars</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The main landmark is the Church of the Assumption of the Virgin Mary. The original Gothic building of the mid-14th century was replaced by the present structure around 1500, burned down in 1605 and 1683 and was reconstructed, and was rebuilt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> style in the 1730s, with its Gothic core preserved.</p><p>Mikulčice-Valy is the main tourist attraction and is freely accessible. An exhibition of archaeological finds from the area is administered by the Masaryk Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hodonin">Hodonín</a>, and the site has been a national cultural monument since 1962. The local economy rests mainly on agriculture and tourism. The village was heavily damaged by the 2021 South Moravia tornado.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mikulčice” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Great Moravia's capital has never been found: the sources name a Veligrad, and <b>Mikulčice</b>, with its palace and twelve churches on an island in the Morava, is the most widely accepted candidate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/great-moravia">Great Moravia · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
